**Record — after:**

```ts
export type Cell = [number, number];

const SURVIVAL_NEIGHBOUR_COUNT = 2;

function isNeighbour([x, y]: Cell, [otherX, otherY]: Cell): boolean {
  const sameCell = x === otherX && y === otherY;
  return !sameCell && Math.abs(x - otherX) <= 1 && Math.abs(y - otherY) <= 1;
}

function livingNeighbourCount(cell: Cell, livingCells: Cell[]): number {
  return livingCells.filter((other) => isNeighbour(cell, other)).length;
}

function survives(cell: Cell, livingCells: Cell[]): boolean {
  return livingNeighbourCount(cell, livingCells) === SURVIVAL_NEIGHBOUR_COUNT;
}

export function nextGeneration(livingCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {
  return livingCells.filter((cell) => survives(cell, livingCells));
}
```

**Retained.** `nextGeneration` now reads as pure orchestration — *keep the living cells that survive* — with the survival policy locatable behind its own domain name. Fewest Elements check: `survives` is one added element, justified under Rule 2 outranking Rule 4 because survival is an independently-changing policy axis distinct from candidate enumeration; it is a semantic seam, not symmetry.

